===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|banshie||dated}}
* {{alter|en|benshee||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|ga|bean sí}}, from {{der|en|sga|ben síde|lit=woman of the fairy mound}}. The term {{m|en||banshee}} entered English in 1771.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bænˈʃiː/|/ˈbænʃiː/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-banshee.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|iː|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Irish and Scottish|_|folklore}} A female [[spirit]], usually taking the form of a woman whose [[mournful]] [[wailing]] warns of an [[impend]]ing death.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1810|publisher={{w|Walter Scott}}|title=The Lady of the Lake|section=3.VII
|passage=Late had he heard, in prophet's dream, / The fatal '''Ben-Shie''''s boding scream {{...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|derogatory}} A [[noisy]] or [[ill-tempered]] [[woman]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=[[w:John T. McIntyre|John Thomas McIntyre]]|title=Steps Going Down|page=15|passage=Where's this old '''banshee''' that runs the place?}}

====Usage notes====
* A banshee was originally merely a [[fairy]] woman who sang a [[caoin|caoineadh]] ([[lament]]) for recently-deceased members of certain families. Translations of Irish works into English made a distinction between the banshee and other fairy folk that the original language and original stories do not seem to have, but from which sprung the current image of the banshee.

